{"report_id":"R0062","domain":"D07","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"The paper evaluates a four-node prototype deployed on airborne embedded computing and communication devices and tests node unavailability by disabling and recovering a UAV's communication program. However, it documents no actual flight-network validation, 3D mobility testing, or live air-to-air channel dynamics, instead listing 3D mobility, unstable A2A links, and harsh environments as unresolved practical challenges.","locators":[{"page":5,"section":"Performance Evaluation","quote":"a set of typical airborne embedded devices"},{"page":6,"section":"Effect of UAV Unavailability","quote":"communication program of UAV#3 was disabled"},{"page":6,"section":"Challenges and Research Directions — Main Technical Challenges","quote":"A2A wireless links, limited battery supply"}]}
{"report_id":"R0062","domain":"D08","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"The paper explicitly frames its evaluation as a preliminary proof-of-concept on a self-built airborne embedded system and marks network status discovery, multi-hop unavailability response, and large-scale hierarchical swarm coordination as open challenges and future work rather than completed capabilities. However, its architectural and concluding claims about handling unstable A2A links and battlefield network fluctuations still extend beyond what the small four-node proof-of-concept directly demonstrated.","locators":[{"page":5,"section":"Performance Evaluation","quote":"a proof-of-concept evaluation is conducted to validate the feasibility"},{"page":6,"section":"Challenges and Research Directions — Main Technical Challenges","quote":"needs to be solved in advance"},{"page":7,"section":"Conclusion","quote":"The preliminary evaluation over the self-built airborne embedded system"}]}
{"report_id":"R0063","domain":"D04","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Section 5.1.3 inventories six baselines spanning general MARL, diffusion, LLM-based, and domain-specific RL methods, and Table 4 in Section 5.2.1 reports the principal comparison across those baselines even though the prose refers to five baselines. However, the listed comparators do not share one uniform test protocol: the Section 5.2.1 cross-play study and subsequent scalability comparison restrict evaluation to a selected subset of top-performing baselines, and matched tuning parity across heterogeneous paradigms is not established.","locators":[{"page":11,"section":"5.1.3 Baseline algorithms","quote":"We compared our framework against six state-of-the-art"},{"page":13,"section":"5.2.1 Swarm combat effectiveness","quote":"our Brain-Torso setup against five baselines"},{"page":13,"section":"5.2.1 Swarm combat effectiveness","quote":"the top-performing baselines against our diverse Adversarial Pool"}]}
{"report_id":"R0063","domain":"D06","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Independent trial averaging and mean-plus-std dispersion are explicitly reported for the principal contrast experiment in Section 5.2.1 (Table 4), alongside targeted sensitivity, ablation, and variance analyses in later subsections. However, the stated independent trial count is qualified to the Table 4 evaluation rather than documented across the cross-play, scalability, latency, and ablation experiments, dispersion is omitted from several secondary result tables, and multi-seed RL training protocols are not reported.","locators":[{"page":13,"section":"5.2.1 Swarm combat effectiveness","quote":"Table 4 pulls together the averages from 500 independent trials."},{"page":13,"section":"Table 4","quote":"Combat performance statistics in scenario B and C (mean ± std)."},{"page":12,"section":"5.1.5 Configurations","quote":null}]}
{"report_id":"R0063","domain":"D07","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Section 5.1 pairs a non-linear 6-DOF flight dynamics simulator (JSBSim) with split workstation-and-edge hardware and synthetic communication-delay and interference models, which adds realism over point-mass abstractions. However, all flight dynamics and network conditions remain simulated rather than physically flight-tested, and Section 6 explicitly acknowledges that real-world cascaded transmission across large swarms would accumulate multi-hop latency that could degrade aerodynamic stability, leaving transfer to physical deployment unverified.","locators":[{"page":11,"section":"5.1.1 Simulation platform","quote":"built our environment on the JSBSim flight dynamics engine"},{"page":11,"section":"5.1.1 Simulation platform","quote":"the communication between the cloud and the edge as a Gaussian"},{"page":20,"section":"6 Conclusion","quote":"total swarm autonomy in real-world environments remains long."},{"page":20,"section":"6 Conclusion","quote":"real-world cascaded transmission across a large swarm"}]}
{"report_id":"R0068","domain":"D06","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Section VI-B presents episode-based convergence curves (Figure 4, plus Figures 6(a) and 7) and sensitivity sweeps across bandwidth, UAV memory, dynamic swarm and memory changes, swarm size, and area size. However, these convergence curves reflect training episodes rather than documented independent repeated runs, and Section VI reports no random-seed counts, dispersion measures, or confidence intervals across repeated trials.","locators":[{"page":10,"section":"VI-B-1 Convergence to the Maximum Rewards","quote":"compare the proposed solutions with respect to the convergence of algorithms to the maximum expected rewards"},{"page":11,"section":"Figure 4","quote":"Comparison of convergence between meta-RL and traditional RL-based solutions toward the maximum expected rewards"},{"page":10,"section":"VI-A Simulations Setup","quote":null}]}
{"report_id":"R0068","domain":"D08","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"The paper explicitly labels its evaluation as simulation-based in Section VI and identifies untested communication factors (channel state information and the Doppler effect) for future work in Section VII. At the same time, results commentary frames performance broadly in terms of suitability for real-world IIoT applications and practical viability beyond what the idealized simulation setup directly verifies.","locators":[{"page":10,"section":"VI. Simulation Results and Analysis","quote":"we present the simulation results that assess the performance of our proposed solutions."},{"page":11,"section":"VI-B-2 Effect of Bandwidths","quote":"reinforcing its suitability for real-world applications"},{"page":13,"section":"VII. Conclusion","quote":"For future work, we plan to investigate the impact of channel state information and the Doppler effect on communication quality."}]}
{"report_id":"R0068","domain":"D10","judgment":"PARTIAL","rationale":"Research funding is disclosed in the page 1 author/title footnote, Open Access publication funding is disclosed in the page 15 end matter, and Section VII notes untested communication factors (channel state information and the Doppler effect) for future work. However, a dedicated limitations discussion is brief, and no conflict-of-interest statement appears in the end matter, which is a reporting absence without implying either conflict or absence of conflict.","locators":[{"page":1,"section":"Title/Author Footnote","quote":"This work was supported in part by Luxembourg National Research Fund through the Project RUTINE under Grant C22/IS/17220888."},{"page":13,"section":"VII. Conclusion","quote":"For future work, we plan to investigate the impact of channel state information and the Doppler effect on communication quality."},{"page":15,"section":"End Matter","quote":"Open Access funding provided by 'Qatar National Library' within the CRUI CARE Agreement"},{"page":15,"section":"End Matter (Conflict of Interest)","quote":null}]}
{"report_id":"R0073","domain":"D05","judgment":"YES","rationale":"The evaluation metrics directly align with the formulated latency-minimization and resilience objectives: Section 5.1 defines end-to-end latency from image acquisition through distributed UAV inference to ground-device delivery, and Section 5.2 evaluates end-to-end latency both under normal operation and under inoperable-UAV scenarios to verify layer-reallocation mission continuity.","locators":[{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings","quote":"This evaluation metric measures the total time required for an image acquired in a hot cell"},{"page":11,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"The end-to-end latency in a scenario with inoperable UAVs is shown in Figure 6."},{"page":11,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"model can maintain mission continuity by reallocating layers to the remaining UAVs"}]}
